17592186413393 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413393 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413393 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c2afbe9-632c-56a2-8877-4a2cf3a61d97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413393 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-7\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(-1-(-1))+\\frac{1}{6}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413394 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413394 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413394 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413394 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413394 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d1f9132-9d3c-569e-af11-701e82cf296a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413394 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x)=F(x)\n$$\n\n \nfor all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f$ has period $\\frac{\\pi }{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+2\\pi )=f(x+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413395 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413395 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413395 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413395 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $G(x)=x+1$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $g(x)$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G(x)$ from $x\\in [0,4)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $g(x+8)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $g(x+2)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413395 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e155a024-962b-5f82-95d5-406e9c9d0535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413395 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $g$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G,$ we have \n\n- $g(x+4)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,4).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x+8)=g((x+4)+4)=g(x+4)=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=5.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(5)=g(1+4)=g(1)=G(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G(5)=5+1=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x)\\ne G(x)$ for $x=5.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=1.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1+2)=g(3)=G(3)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1)=G(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x+2)\\ne g(x)$ for $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
